    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Periods are in, and `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**What it does**\n- **Adding a period:** there's a new Periods section on the screen with a name, a from date and a to date. A period is refused, with the message in `period-form-error`, if its name is already used on the sheet, if it ends before it starts, or if its dates overlap another period. A refused period writes nothing to the store or the audit file.\n- **Audit:** an accepted period writes `PERIOD_ADDED sheet=\u2026 by=\u2026 period=<name> from=<date> to=<date>`.\n- **Claims:** each claim row shows the name of its period in `claim-period`, or nothing if its date isn't in any period.\n- **Period due-total:** each row (`period-row-<name>`) shows `period-due-total`: the dues of the OPEN and SETTLED claims dated in that period, added up the same way a claim's due is shown. Claims with no due (\"n/a\") are skipped, and a period with none shows 0.00.\n\n**How it's built**\n- **Server:** a new `period` table (migration `V14__period.sql`), with the code under `java/.../app/period/` and two endpoints, `GET` and `POST /api/sheets/{sheetId}/periods`.\n- **Concurrent adds:** adding a period locks the sheet first (a new `SheetService.lock`), so two users adding at the same moment can't create overlapping periods.\n- **Front end:** the new `features/periods/` component is placed in `People`, next to Rates. The claim's period and the due-totals are worked out in the browser, the same way the existing per-person due-totals are.\n\n**Choices I made where the request was silent**\n- Names are compared ignoring case, as person names already are, so \"p1\" counts as a duplicate of \"P1\".\n- A period that ends before it starts is caught on the screen before anything is sent. The server checks it too.\n- A claim of someone whose grade has no rate today still adds its due to its period. On the person's own row that person's due-total shows \"n/a\", so the period total and the overall due-total could differ in that case. The spec doesn't test this.",
